## Codebase Discoveries
- Upstream's organizing thesis is that every gh stack invocation must be
  non-interactive. It omits `gh stack modify`, `switch`, and `alias` entirely
  because all three hard-error without a tty:
    cmd/switch.go:46-49 and cmd/modify.go:276-279 both check
    !cfg.IsInteractive() and return ErrSilent.
  Our old skill taught `gh stack modify` as the PRIMARY restructuring workflow
  (old SKILL.md:167-184). Upstream teaches unstack + re-init as the only one.
  Anything that relied on our modify guidance will now find nothing.
- Upstream exit code 10 says "This skill does not use modify, so agents should
  not produce this."
- One upstream claim is contradicted by its own source. The skill states "If a
  command would prompt for input, it will hang indefinitely." Interactivity is
  actually decided by internal/config/config.go:129-131
  (ForceInteractive || Terminal.IsTerminalOutput()), and every prompt site
  degrades rather than hangs: view.go:80 skips the TUI, submit.go:203 acts as
  --auto, merge.go:162 skips confirmation, init.go:132 errors on missing args,
  checkout.go:441 prints the divergence hint and exits 3. In a piped shell
  nothing hangs. The "always pass the flag" rules are still correct habit --
  they make behavior identical with or without a pty -- but do not trust the
  hang claim when debugging.
- Exit codes in cmd/utils.go:27-38 matched both the old and new skill exactly,
  including 9 (ErrStacksUnavailable) and 10 (ErrModifyRecovery). The 100-PR
  limit and unsupported auto-merge/rule-bypass are confirmed in
  docs/src/content/docs/faq.md:38 and :184.

